1
00:04:05,830 --> 00:04:07,200
If I may, nephew,

2
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,750
I encountered a situation with one of my lieutenants

3
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:11,850
at the Stone Mill which may have some bearing...

4
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:15,550
Why don't you shut your mouth about that damned mill?

5
00:04:15,550 --> 00:04:18,290
And don't call him "Nephew." He is your king.

6
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:19,690
Robb knows I meant him no disr...

7
00:04:19,690 --> 00:04:23,110
You're lucky I'm not your king.

8
00:04:23,430 --> 00:04:27,250
I wouldn't let you wave your blunders around like a victory flag.

9
00:04:27,250 --> 00:04:29,780
My blunder sent Tywin's mad dog scurrying back

10
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,650
to Casterly Rock with his tail between his legs.

11
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:35,020
I think King Robb understands we're not gonna win this war

12
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:36,830
if he's the only one winning any battles.

13
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:38,040
No, there's glory enough to go around.

14
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:40,160
It's not about glory.

15
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,470
Your instructions were to wait for him to come to you.

16
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:45,810
I seized an opportunity.

17
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:47,770
What value was the mill?

18
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:50,200
The Mountain was garrisoned across the river from it.

19
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:51,520
Is he there now?

20
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:53,830
Of course not. We took the fight to him.

21
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:55,200
He could not withstand us.

22
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:58,440
I wanted to draw the Mountain into the west,

23
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:01,890
into our country where we could surround him and kill him.

24
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:04,630
I wanted him to chase us,

25
00:05:04,930 --> 00:05:08,060
which he would have done because he is a mad dog

26
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:10,260
without a strategic thought in his head.

27
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:13,500
I could have that head on a spike by now.

28
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:17,580
Instead, I have a mill.

29
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:22,780
We took hostages.

30
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:26,420
Willem Lannister. Martyn Lannister.

31
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:28,570
Willem and Martyn Lannister are 14 years old.

32
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:31,870
Martyn is 15, I believe.

33
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,990
Tywin Lannister has my sisters.

34
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,960
Have I sued for peace?

35
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:42,320
No.

36
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:43,660
Do you think he'll sue for peace

37
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:47,230
because we have his father's

38
00:05:47,230 --> 00:05:50,280
brother's great-grandsons?

39
00:05:54,670 --> 00:05:55,840
No.

40
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,160
How many men did you lose?

41
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:00,830
208.

42
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:02,780
But for every man we lost, the Lannisters...

43
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:06,060
We need our men more than Tywin needs his!

44
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:13,690
I'm sorry.

45
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,490
- I didn't know. - You would have.

46
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:20,180
Right here today at this gathering if you had been patient.

47
00:06:20,290 --> 00:06:23,330
We seem to be running short of patience here.

48
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:24,900
You know who isn't?

49
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,090
Tywin Lannister.

50
00:08:21,210 --> 00:08:23,600
Intimate. Lovely table.

51
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:26,090
Better chairs than the old small council chamber.

52
00:08:26,090 --> 00:08:29,470
Conveniently close to your own quarters. I like it.

53
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:32,340
What news of Jaime?

54
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:38,850
20,000 unwashed Northerners

55
00:08:38,860 --> 00:08:40,730
have known about his escape for weeks.

56
00:08:40,730 --> 00:08:43,560
Collectively, you control more spies and informants

57
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:45,210
than the rest of the world combined.

58
00:08:45,210 --> 00:08:46,930
Do you mean to tell me that none of you

59
00:08:46,930 --> 00:08:48,930
has any notion of where he is?

60
00:08:48,930 --> 00:08:52,570
- We are trying, my lord. - Try harder.

61
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:55,930
What do we have, then?

62
00:08:55,930 --> 00:08:59,530
Robb Stark and most of his bannermen are in Riverrun

63
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:03,490
for the funeral of his grandfather Lord Hoster Tully.

64
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:08,410
In Stark's absence, Roose Bolton holds Harrenhal,

65
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:11,890
which would seem to make him Lord of Harrenhal,

66
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:14,370
- in practice if not in name. - Well, let him have it.

67
00:09:14,370 --> 00:09:18,630
The name suits our purposes far more than that useless pile of rubble.

68
00:09:18,630 --> 00:09:22,020
The Lord of Harrenhal will make a worthy suitor for the widow Arryn.

69
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:25,160
For which I am extremely grateful to you, my lord.

70
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:27,880
Lady Arryn and I have known each other since we were children.

71
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:29,870
She has always been...

72
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:33,180
positively predisposed toward me.

73
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:36,340
A successful courtship would make Lord Baelish

74
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:38,230
acting Lord of the Vale.

75
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:41,060
Titles do seem to breed titles.

76
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,040
You'll leave for the Eyrie as soon as possible

77
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:45,170
and bring Lysa Arryn into the fold.

78
00:09:45,170 --> 00:09:47,920
Then the young wolf can add his own aunt to the list

79
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,800
of people who have taken up arms against him.

80
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:52,990
Far be it from me to hinder true love,

81
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,100
but Lord Baelish's absence would present certain problems.

82
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:59,260
The royal wedding may end up

83
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:02,160
being the most expensive event in living memory.

84
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:05,440
Summer has ended, hard days lie ahead.

85
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:09,490
Not a good time to leave the crown's finances unattended.

86
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,020
Fully agreed.

87
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:14,960
Which is why I'm naming you new Master of Coin.

88
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:16,500
Master of Coin?

89
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:19,450
It would appear to be a position that best suits your talents.

90
00:10:19,450 --> 00:10:20,860
I'm quite good at spending money,

91
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:23,040
but a lifetime of outrageous wealth

92
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:24,840
hasn't taught me much about managing it.

93
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:28,360
I have no doubt you will prove equal to this challenge.

94
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:30,450
Hear, hear.

95
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:33,730
*He lifted her high in the air*

96
00:10:33,730 --> 00:10:35,850
*He sniffed and roared and smelled her there*

97
00:10:35,850 --> 00:10:38,190
*She kicked and wailed, a maid so fair*

98
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:41,100
*But he licked the honey from her hair*

99
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:43,370
*From there to here, from here to there*

100
00:10:43,370 --> 00:10:45,780
*All black and brown and covered with hair*

101
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:47,900
*He smelled that girl in the summer air*

102
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:50,660
*The bear, the bear and the maiden fair...*

103
00:10:52,210 --> 00:10:54,110
I hope you're pleased.

104
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:56,240
If you had armed me, they never would have taken us.

105
00:10:56,240 --> 00:10:58,070
You were armed when we were taken.

106
00:10:58,070 --> 00:11:00,230
I was in chains if you recall.

107
00:11:00,570 --> 00:11:02,170
Our little match would have ended quite quick

108
00:11:02,170 --> 00:11:03,830
if my hands weren't bound.

109
00:11:04,170 --> 00:11:07,260
All my life I've been hearing, "Jaime Lannister,

110
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:09,500
what a brilliant swordsman."

111
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:12,390
You were slower than I expected.

112
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,060
And more predictable.

113
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:15,810
I've been sitting in a muddy pen

114
00:11:15,810 --> 00:11:17,620
wrapped in chains for the past year.

115
00:11:17,620 --> 00:11:19,980
And I'm a woman. I was still beating you.

116
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:21,690
You were not beating me.

117
00:11:21,690 --> 00:11:24,240
Maybe you were as good as people said...

118
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:25,800
once.

119
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:29,820
Or maybe people just love to overpraise a famous name.

120
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,810
When we make camp tonight, you'll be raped.

121
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:37,290
More than once.

122
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:40,860
None of these fellows have ever been with a noblewoman.

123
00:11:43,140 --> 00:11:44,800
You'd be wise not to resist.

124
00:11:44,810 --> 00:11:46,640
- Would I? - They'll knock your teeth out.

125
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:47,910
You think I care about my teeth?

126
00:11:47,910 --> 00:11:50,680
No, I don't think you care about your teeth.

127
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:56,370
If you fight them, they will kill you.

128
00:11:56,370 --> 00:11:57,800
Do you understand?

129
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:00,710
I'm the prisoner of value, not you.

130
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:03,370
Let them have what they want. What does it matter?

131
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:05,480
- What does it matter? - Close your eyes.

132
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,100
Pretend they're Renly.

133
00:12:09,310 --> 00:12:11,640
If you were a woman, you wouldn't resist?

134
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:14,000
You'd let them do what they wanted?

135
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,920
If I was a woman, I'd make them kill me.

136
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:19,150
But I'm not, thank the gods.

137
00:12:23,770 --> 00:12:25,940
If I had a proper forge, I could make it good as new.

138
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:27,660
Doesn't have to look pretty.

139
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:30,190
It just has to keep the arrows out of my heart.

140
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:31,900
Why are you helping him?

141
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:34,330
He takes us prisoner and now he's our friend?

142
00:12:34,330 --> 00:12:36,180
You're not our prisoner, little lady.

143
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:38,390
- What am I? - Our guest.

144
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:40,700
No one's put any chains on you.

145
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:42,510
So I can walk away, then?

146
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:45,120
These woods aren't safe for Ned Stark's daughter.

147
00:12:45,150 --> 00:12:46,760
You're lucky we found you.

148
00:12:51,350 --> 00:12:54,870
You think you're good with that bow, you little twat?

149
00:12:54,870 --> 00:12:56,520
Better than anyone you've ever met.

150
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,000
A coward's weapon.

151
00:12:58,490 --> 00:13:00,430
I like to fight up close.

152
00:13:00,430 --> 00:13:03,940
I like to see a man's face when I put the steel in him.

153
00:13:03,940 --> 00:13:06,210
Why? So you can kiss him?

154
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:08,540
You remember the last time you were here?

155
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:12,060
Looks like every other shit inn on the road.

156
00:13:12,060 --> 00:13:14,840
Now, apologies, but you're one ugly fucker

157
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:17,070
and I'd rather not see you no more.

158
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:23,030
Watch your head.

159
00:13:24,170 --> 00:13:24,940
Off we go.

160
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:25,950
Walk up!

161
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:34,830
What are you doing? We're about to leave.

162
00:13:34,960 --> 00:13:36,210
I'm staying.

163
00:13:38,230 --> 00:13:39,350
You're what?

164
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:42,790
I baked some brown bread for the innkeep

165
00:13:42,790 --> 00:13:44,610
and she said she never had better.

166
00:13:44,630 --> 00:13:46,240
Told Thoros she's keeping me as a payment

167
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:48,100
for all the free meals she's given him.

168
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:50,270
Anyway, it's not me the Brotherhood wants.

169
00:13:50,390 --> 00:13:51,900
My brother ain't no king.

170
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:54,350
I'm not a Stark of Winterhell.

171
00:13:54,350 --> 00:13:55,820
Winterfell.

172
00:13:56,680 --> 00:13:59,270
- You sure? - I'm sure.

173
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:04,290
Well, I made you something.

174
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:10,480
What is it?

175
00:14:10,880 --> 00:14:12,210
It's a wolf.

176
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:18,700
Yes, it is.

177
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:21,600
That's the tail?

178
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:22,880
Yeah.

179
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:27,420
Well, be safe.

180
00:14:27,550 --> 00:14:28,840
Yeah, you, too.

181
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:31,160
Don't get stabbed.

182
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:33,670
You don't...

183
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:35,670
burn your fingers.

184
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:47,590
Good-bye, Hot Pie.

185
00:14:48,870 --> 00:14:50,310
Good-bye, Arry.

186
00:15:01,890 --> 00:15:03,760
Up you go, little lady.

187
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:11,870
Hey, Hot Pie.

188
00:15:14,870 --> 00:15:16,520
It's really good.

189
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:25,260
A person could almost be forgiven

190
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:27,510
for forgetting we're at war.

191
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:34,520
It often comforts me to think that even in war's darkest days,

192
00:15:34,860 --> 00:15:36,640
in most places in the world

193
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:39,830
absolutely nothing is happening.

194
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:45,070
I've missed you, Uncle.

195
00:15:45,370 --> 00:15:46,920
Father missed you, too,

196
00:15:47,430 --> 00:15:48,830
from the day you left.

197
00:15:49,010 --> 00:15:51,160
Maybe he never said it in so many words...

198
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:52,390
Maybe?

199
00:15:53,010 --> 00:15:55,530
Your father was a stubborn old ox.

200
00:15:55,530 --> 00:15:57,090
I was surprised when he died.

201
00:15:57,090 --> 00:15:59,600
Didn't think death had the patience.

202
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:03,810
I'm glad you were with him.

203
00:16:04,830 --> 00:16:06,930
I wish to the gods I had been.

204
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:12,110
Did you make peace in the end?

205
00:16:12,110 --> 00:16:14,720
After 30 years of fighting,

206
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:17,310
I don't think he remembered what started it.

207
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:20,910
He asked me to stop calling myself Blackfish.

208
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:23,340
He said it was an old joke

209
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:26,120
and it was never funny to begin with.

210
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:29,550
I told him people had been calling me Blackfish

211
00:16:29,550 --> 00:16:33,010
for so long, they don't remember my real name.

212
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:38,690
Every time he would leave for the capital

213
00:16:38,690 --> 00:16:40,110
or fight in a campaign,

214
00:16:41,750 --> 00:16:43,010
I'd see him off.

215
00:16:44,270 --> 00:16:46,660
"Wait for me, little Cat," He'd say.

216
00:16:48,380 --> 00:16:49,430
"Wait for me

217
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:52,510
and I'll come back to you."

218
00:16:54,060 --> 00:16:56,100
And I would sit at this window

219
00:16:56,100 --> 00:16:58,720
every day when the sun came up, waiting.

220
00:17:02,510 --> 00:17:03,470
I wonder

221
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:07,970
how many times did Bran or Rickon

222
00:17:07,970 --> 00:17:09,830
stare across the moors of Winterfell

223
00:17:09,830 --> 00:17:11,650
waiting for me to return.

224
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:14,810
I will never see them again.

225
00:17:18,330 --> 00:17:19,890
You mustn't think it.

226
00:17:21,150 --> 00:17:22,510
We don't know the truth.

227
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:24,840
They could be in hiding.

228
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:30,680
Robb believes they're alive.

229
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:34,130
And he must go on believing.

230
00:17:34,130 --> 00:17:36,680
He's got to remain strong if he's to prevail.

231
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:40,120
And you must remain strong for him.

232
00:17:52,150 --> 00:17:53,600
You're Robb Stark's wife.

233
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:55,690
Hold still.

234
00:17:57,710 --> 00:17:59,240
Is it true what they say about him?

235
00:17:59,950 --> 00:18:02,340
I don't know. What do they say about him?

236
00:18:02,750 --> 00:18:04,570
That he can turn into a wolf at night.

237
00:18:06,450 --> 00:18:07,220
True.

238
00:18:07,790 --> 00:18:09,720
And he eats the flesh of his enemies.

239
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:11,120
True.

240
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:16,820
You're a Lannister, aren't you?

241
00:18:16,820 --> 00:18:17,890
Martyn Lannister.

242
00:18:18,350 --> 00:18:19,660
Martyn Lannister.

243
00:18:21,790 --> 00:18:23,210
You've nothing to fear.

244
00:18:23,210 --> 00:18:24,830
My husband doesn't eat children.

245
00:18:26,870 --> 00:18:29,210
Unless it's a full moon.

246
00:18:29,210 --> 00:18:30,980
It's not a full moon tonight, is it?

247
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:32,640
See?

248
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,080
Nothing to fear.

249
00:18:54,230 --> 00:18:56,300
Always the artists.

250
00:19:16,180 --> 00:19:17,390
It's only horses.

251
00:19:18,980 --> 00:19:19,800
No men.

252
00:19:22,150 --> 00:19:23,830
You said there was dead crows.

253
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:25,130
There was.

254
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:29,550
How many men were here?

255
00:19:30,090 --> 00:19:31,310
About 300.

256
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:34,620
And you know what those men are now?

257
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:41,390
We're all the same to them-- meat for their army.

258
00:19:43,190 --> 00:19:44,510
Do you think anyone got away?

259
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:46,910
It's not impossible.

260
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:49,600
You don't go far betting against Mormont.

261
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,180
But dead or alive,

262
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,440
he took a big gamble coming north.

263
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:56,260
And he lost.

264
00:19:56,260 --> 00:19:57,900
His best fighting men are dead.

265
00:19:58,750 --> 00:20:01,710
And whether he's Lord Commander of the Night's Watch

266
00:20:02,200 --> 00:20:04,060
or a blue-eyed corpse,

267
00:20:04,510 --> 00:20:06,300
he's a long way from home.

268
00:20:07,610 --> 00:20:08,400
Tormund.

269
00:20:09,230 --> 00:20:10,360
Climb the Wall.

270
00:20:11,770 --> 00:20:13,640
Take Orell and 20 good men.

271
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:15,070
And take this one.

272
00:20:15,690 --> 00:20:18,770
He knows Castle Black's defenses better than any of us.

273
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:20,570
And if he's useful, good. If not...

274
00:20:21,040 --> 00:20:22,430
throw him off the Wall.

275
00:20:23,290 --> 00:20:25,120
See if crows can fly.

276
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:29,780
We're finally going to war, old friend?

277
00:20:29,780 --> 00:20:31,380
Hide near Castle Black.

278
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:33,930
When I give the signal, hit them in the night.

279
00:20:33,930 --> 00:20:36,360
They've got a big old wall to hide behind,

280
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,100
but it only guards one side.

281
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:41,130
- We'll meet again. - Aye.

282
00:20:41,590 --> 00:20:43,560
- If you do your job. - Ah.

283
00:20:44,590 --> 00:20:46,030
How will we see your signal?

284
00:20:46,330 --> 00:20:48,800
Send your eagle above the Wall every night.

285
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:49,560
When it's time,

286
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:53,090
I'm going to light the biggest fire the North has ever seen.

287
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:21,550
Ghost?

288
00:21:35,830 --> 00:21:39,170
What have we here? Frozen crows?

289
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:40,890
We've come a long way.

290
00:21:43,930 --> 00:21:46,100
Smaller flock than you went north with.

291
00:21:47,180 --> 00:21:48,690
We can talk inside.

292
00:21:48,930 --> 00:21:51,370
Oh, can we?

293
00:22:12,580 --> 00:22:14,230
They need some water.

294
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:16,790
Tether it and I'll be inside.

295
00:22:32,150 --> 00:22:33,810
Keep your eyes where they belong.

296
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:36,120
They're not for you.

297
00:22:36,610 --> 00:22:39,240
Bet you feed that pig better than you feed us.

298
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:42,210
Aye, pig's got value to me.

299
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:46,110
You should all be kissing my feet for letting you in.

300
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:50,960
I'd have turned you all away if I wasn't a godly man.

301
00:22:51,520 --> 00:22:53,080
You are a godly man?

302
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:56,740
I am. I got no fear of what's out there.

303
00:22:57,230 --> 00:22:58,900
When the white cold comes,

304
00:22:58,900 --> 00:23:02,340
your swords and cloaks and bloody fires won't help you.

305
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,380
The only ones left will be those who are right with the gods.

306
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:09,080
The real gods.

307
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:17,050
Go tell her she can bite down on a rag

308
00:23:17,050 --> 00:23:19,620
or she can bite down on my fist.

309
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:23,830
Women.

310
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:30,720
That sow right there gave birth to a litter of eight.

311
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:31,890
Barely a grunt.

312
00:23:33,540 --> 00:23:36,200
And she's almost as fat as this one here.

313
00:23:37,360 --> 00:23:39,050
Now why don't you dine on him?

314
00:23:39,690 --> 00:23:41,790
Carve off what you need as you go.

315
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:45,180
Well, look at him. He's a walking feast.

316
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,240
Here we are.

317
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:07,100
Sharpen these for me, would ya?

318
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:27,340
I see the head. Push.

319
00:24:27,890 --> 00:24:28,700
That's it.

320
00:24:30,120 --> 00:24:31,240
You're nearly there.

321
00:24:36,990 --> 00:24:38,760
One more push should do it.

322
00:24:48,340 --> 00:24:50,890
What is it? What is it?

323
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:23,850
Keep quiet.

324
00:25:53,880 --> 00:25:56,950
If you can't ride, we don't stand a chance.

325
00:25:57,480 --> 00:25:58,490
I can ride.

326
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:00,070
You're sure?

327
00:26:14,870 --> 00:26:17,520
Ride east. Follow the rising sun.

328
00:26:17,970 --> 00:26:19,360
Your sister's waiting for you.

329
00:26:19,910 --> 00:26:21,990
I'll make you a Lord of the Iron Islands for this.

330
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:23,620
We're not in the Iron Islands.

331
00:26:24,130 --> 00:26:25,910
- Go. - Come on.

332
00:26:26,190 --> 00:26:27,580
Come on. Come on.

333
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:28,710
Come on. Come on.

334
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:34,620
Come on back.

335
00:26:39,230 --> 00:26:41,140
You refuse to tell me where you're going?

336
00:26:41,470 --> 00:26:43,890
I don't know yet. The fires will show me.

337
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:47,120
- How long will you be gone? - I don't know.

338
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:49,940
You're abandoning me.

339
00:26:50,050 --> 00:26:51,990
I will never abandon you.

340
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:56,030
You are the Son of Fire.

341
00:26:56,030 --> 00:26:58,810
- I am sworn to serve you. - Then serve me.

342
00:26:58,810 --> 00:27:00,510
When I return, you will understand.

343
00:27:03,300 --> 00:27:05,340
My enemies think they've destroyed me.

344
00:27:07,740 --> 00:27:08,990
They're laughing at me

345
00:27:10,020 --> 00:27:11,730
the way Renly laughed at me.

346
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:14,880
I want Joffrey dead.

347
00:27:15,950 --> 00:27:18,080
I want Robb Stark dead.

348
00:27:21,970 --> 00:27:23,380
Make me another son.

349
00:27:23,820 --> 00:27:24,690
I cannot.

350
00:27:26,070 --> 00:27:26,790
Why?

351
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:30,020
You don't have the strength. It would kill you.

352
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:32,840
I'm not so easily killed. Men have been trying for years.

353
00:27:37,750 --> 00:27:38,920
I want you.

354
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:55,400
Your fires burn low, my king.

355
00:27:59,820 --> 00:28:01,680
There is another way. A better way.

356
00:28:03,550 --> 00:28:05,820
You told me your magic requires a king's blood.

357
00:28:05,820 --> 00:28:07,570
- Yes. - I'm the one true king.

358
00:28:07,570 --> 00:28:08,320
You are.

359
00:28:09,160 --> 00:28:11,840
But there are others with your blood in their veins.

360
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:16,740
You will sit on the Iron Throne,

361
00:28:16,740 --> 00:28:19,060
but first there must be sacrifices.

362
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:20,970
The Lord of Light demands it.

363
00:28:33,130 --> 00:28:35,690
The Walk of Punishment is a warning, Your Grace.

364
00:28:35,690 --> 00:28:36,560
To whom?

365
00:28:37,060 --> 00:28:40,370
To any slave who contemplates doing whatever these slaves did.

366
00:28:41,670 --> 00:28:42,920
Give me your water.

367
00:28:44,140 --> 00:28:47,750
Khaleesi, this man has been sentenced to death.

368
00:28:53,640 --> 00:28:54,890
Here, drink.

369
00:28:58,710 --> 00:29:00,080
Leave this place, Your Grace.

370
00:29:00,630 --> 00:29:01,980
Leave tonight, I beg you.

371
00:29:02,570 --> 00:29:04,570
And what is she to do for soldiers?

372
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:06,550
We can find sellswords in Pentos and Myr.

373
00:29:07,030 --> 00:29:09,260
Is it "We" Already, Ser Barristan?

374
00:29:11,100 --> 00:29:13,450
If you want to sit on the throne your ancestors built,

375
00:29:13,450 --> 00:29:14,500
you must win it.

376
00:29:14,500 --> 00:29:16,920
That will mean blood on your hands before the thing is done.

377
00:29:16,920 --> 00:29:20,240
The blood of my enemies, not the blood of innocents.

378
00:29:23,160 --> 00:29:25,480
How many wars have you fought in, Ser Barristan?

379
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:26,230
Three.

380
00:29:26,720 --> 00:29:27,630
Have you ever seen a war

381
00:29:27,630 --> 00:29:30,050
where innocents didn't die by the thousands?

382
00:29:35,180 --> 00:29:37,910
I was in King's Landing after the sack, Khaleesi.

383
00:29:39,020 --> 00:29:41,300
You know what I saw? Butchery.

384
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:43,630
Babies, children, old men.

385
00:29:43,630 --> 00:29:45,430
More women raped than you can count.

386
00:29:45,880 --> 00:29:47,720
There's a beast in every man

387
00:29:47,720 --> 00:29:50,240
and it stirs when you put a sword in his hand.

388
00:29:51,030 --> 00:29:53,020
But the Unsullied are not men.

389
00:29:53,350 --> 00:29:54,750
They do not rape.

390
00:29:54,750 --> 00:29:57,860
They do not put cities to the sword unless they're ordered to do so.

391
00:29:57,860 --> 00:29:58,980
If you buy them,

392
00:29:59,470 --> 00:30:02,450
the only men they'll kill are those you want dead.

393
00:30:02,450 --> 00:30:04,070
Do you disagree, Ser Barristan?

394
00:30:04,070 --> 00:30:07,010
When your brother Rhaegar led his army into battle at the Trident,

395
00:30:07,510 --> 00:30:09,980
men died for him because they believed in him,

396
00:30:09,990 --> 00:30:10,980
because they loved him,

397
00:30:11,740 --> 00:30:14,090
not because they'd been bought at a slaver's auction.

398
00:30:15,580 --> 00:30:18,710
I fought beside the last dragon on that day, Your Grace.

399
00:30:20,040 --> 00:30:21,400
I bled beside him.

400
00:30:22,460 --> 00:30:25,960
Rhaegar fought valiantly, Rhaegar fought nobly,

401
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:28,070
and Rhaegar died.

402
00:30:30,010 --> 00:30:31,530
Did you know him well, Ser Barristan?

403
00:30:31,530 --> 00:30:35,280
I did, Your Grace. Finest man I ever met.

404
00:30:36,110 --> 00:30:37,420
I wish I had known him.

405
00:30:38,460 --> 00:30:40,360
But he was not the last dragon.

406
00:30:51,490 --> 00:30:55,270
All? Did this one's ears mishear, Your Grace?

407
00:30:55,270 --> 00:30:57,650
They did not. I want to buy them all.

408
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:15,440
There are 8,000 Unsullied in Astapor.

409
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:17,880
Is this what you mean by all?

410
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:19,780
Yes. 8,000.

411
00:31:19,780 --> 00:31:21,530
And the ones still in training as well.

412
00:31:31,590 --> 00:31:35,040
Master Greizhen says they cannot sell half-trained boys.

413
00:31:35,300 --> 00:31:36,780
If they fail on the battlefield,

414
00:31:36,780 --> 00:31:39,170
they will bring shame upon all of Astapor.

415
00:31:39,180 --> 00:31:40,940
I will have them all or take none.

416
00:31:41,520 --> 00:31:42,570
Many will fall in battle.

417
00:31:42,570 --> 00:31:44,900
I'll need the boys to pick up the swords they drop.

418
00:31:50,030 --> 00:31:52,700
Master Kraznys says you cannot afford this.

419
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:59,160
Your ship will buy you 100 Unsullied.

420
00:32:02,250 --> 00:32:04,490
Because Master Kraznys is generous.

421
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:09,180
The gold you have left is worth 10.

422
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:12,720
But good Master Kraznys will give you 20.

423
00:32:15,050 --> 00:32:17,020
The Dothraki you have with you--

424
00:32:21,640 --> 00:32:25,250
The Dothraki you have are not worth what they cost to feed,

425
00:32:25,550 --> 00:32:27,990
but Master Kraznys will give you three Unsullied

426
00:32:27,990 --> 00:32:29,000
for all of them.

427
00:32:31,090 --> 00:32:33,780
Master Kraznys asks how you propose to pay

428
00:32:33,780 --> 00:32:39,210
for the remaining 7,877 Unsullied.

429
00:32:44,490 --> 00:32:47,320
I have dragons. I'll give you one.

430
00:32:51,650 --> 00:32:54,090
You will win the throne with dragons, not slaves, Your Grace.

431
00:32:54,090 --> 00:32:56,350
Khaleesi, please.

432
00:33:05,810 --> 00:33:07,620
Three dragons.

433
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:08,840
- One. - Two.

434
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:09,670
One.

435
00:33:20,930 --> 00:33:22,720
They want the biggest one.

436
00:33:23,290 --> 00:33:25,160
- Done. - Done.

437
00:33:30,270 --> 00:33:32,680
I'll take you as well, now.

438
00:33:33,280 --> 00:33:35,640
You'll be Master Kraznys' gift to me.

439
00:33:35,990 --> 00:33:37,710
A token of a bargain well struck.

440
00:33:48,670 --> 00:33:51,880
Khaleesi, a dragon is worth more than any army.

441
00:33:51,890 --> 00:33:53,590
Aegon Targaryen proved that.

442
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:57,220
You're both here to advise me.

443
00:33:57,220 --> 00:33:58,610
I value your advice,

444
00:33:58,610 --> 00:34:01,560
but if you ever question me in front of strangers again,

445
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:03,070
you'll be advising someone else.

446
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:04,960
Is that understood?

447
00:34:09,160 --> 00:34:10,160
Do you have a name?

448
00:34:10,570 --> 00:34:13,170
This one's name is Missandei, Your Grace.

449
00:34:13,620 --> 00:34:14,650
Do you have a family?

450
00:34:14,650 --> 00:34:17,170
A mother and a father you'd return to if you had the choice?

451
00:34:17,750 --> 00:34:20,410
No, Your Grace. No family living.

452
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:23,930
You belong to me now. It is your duty to tell me the truth.

453
00:34:24,240 --> 00:34:26,500
Yes, Your Grace. Lying is a great offense.

454
00:34:26,940 --> 00:34:30,240
Many of those on the Walk of Punishment were taken there for less.

455
00:34:30,240 --> 00:34:31,530
I offered water to one of the slaves

456
00:34:31,530 --> 00:34:32,780
dying on the Walk of Punishment.

457
00:34:32,780 --> 00:34:35,470
Do you know what he said to me? "Let me die."

458
00:34:36,200 --> 00:34:38,820
There are no masters in the grave, Your Grace.

459
00:34:39,930 --> 00:34:41,540
Is it true what Master Kraznys told me

460
00:34:41,540 --> 00:34:43,850
about the Unsullied? About their obedience?

461
00:34:44,710 --> 00:34:46,820
All questions have been taken from them.

462
00:34:47,200 --> 00:34:49,010
They obey, that is all.

463
00:34:49,470 --> 00:34:51,340
Once they are yours, they are yours.

464
00:34:51,910 --> 00:34:54,450
They will fall on their swords if you command it.

465
00:34:54,690 --> 00:34:57,950
And what about you? You know that I'm taking you to war.

466
00:34:57,950 --> 00:35:01,070
You may go hungry. You may fall sick.

467
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:03,070
You may be killed.

468
00:35:04,410 --> 00:35:06,130
Valar morghulis.

469
00:35:06,130 --> 00:35:08,950
Yes, all men must die.

470
00:35:10,420 --> 00:35:11,840
But we are not men.

471
00:35:22,550 --> 00:35:25,700
A surprising place to keep the royal ledgers.

472
00:35:25,780 --> 00:35:27,800
I'm surprised you're surprised.

473
00:35:29,670 --> 00:35:31,710
This is the safest place in the city.

474
00:35:32,810 --> 00:35:34,320
Not for bastards.

475
00:35:35,960 --> 00:35:37,360
That's all of them, my lord.

476
00:35:37,820 --> 00:35:40,080
Thank you, my dear. Pod.

477
00:35:41,350 --> 00:35:43,850
Pod. Take them outside.

478
00:35:43,850 --> 00:35:45,580
- I'll be there in a moment. - Yes, my lord.

479
00:35:57,670 --> 00:36:00,790
I hear you owe that boy a significant debt.

480
00:36:00,970 --> 00:36:02,140
Only my life.

481
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:04,360
Not all that significant, I'm afraid.

482
00:36:04,360 --> 00:36:05,830
You should have him knighted.

483
00:36:06,450 --> 00:36:09,670
If only the Master of Coin had such power.

484
00:36:10,450 --> 00:36:12,430
If only.

485
00:36:14,730 --> 00:36:16,810
I owe you a significant debt.

486
00:36:16,810 --> 00:36:19,010
Our redheaded friend.

487
00:36:19,350 --> 00:36:22,080
You secured her release when the queen detained her.

488
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:23,450
Oh, that.

489
00:36:24,380 --> 00:36:26,990
Of course. A simple misunderstanding.

490
00:36:26,990 --> 00:36:29,130
Apparently, Her Grace believed

491
00:36:29,130 --> 00:36:32,850
that you two had some sort of special relationship.

492
00:36:32,850 --> 00:36:36,190
We don't. I did fuck her once.

493
00:36:36,190 --> 00:36:38,060
- I know. - But we don't.

494
00:36:38,060 --> 00:36:39,010
I know.

495
00:36:39,790 --> 00:36:41,440
But how would the queen get that idea?

496
00:36:42,210 --> 00:36:43,850
Why don't you ask her?

497
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:49,390
Any advice for me on my new position?

498
00:36:49,920 --> 00:36:51,460
Keep a low profile.

499
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:57,950
If I had a gold dragon for every time I heard that joke,

500
00:36:57,950 --> 00:36:59,200
I'd be richer than you are.

501
00:36:59,200 --> 00:37:00,880
Well, you are richer than I am.

502
00:37:01,320 --> 00:37:02,310
Good point.

503
00:37:03,430 --> 00:37:06,290
They're only numbers. Numbers on paper.

504
00:37:06,290 --> 00:37:10,010
Once you understand that, it's easy to make them behave.

505
00:37:10,240 --> 00:37:11,480
Trivial even.

506
00:37:11,710 --> 00:37:13,170
You want a real challenge?

507
00:37:13,720 --> 00:37:15,120
Try whores.

508
00:37:15,120 --> 00:37:16,730
I've tried quite a few.

509
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:19,920
Well, lots of work to do.

510
00:37:19,920 --> 00:37:21,170
Enjoy the Eyrie.

511
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:23,820
Come on, I'm here later.

512
00:37:24,980 --> 00:37:25,770
Come on.

513
00:37:30,260 --> 00:37:31,330
Tonight, then.

514
00:37:34,260 --> 00:37:35,570
Looks like dull reading.

515
00:37:35,570 --> 00:37:37,700
You think all reading is dull reading.

516
00:37:38,050 --> 00:37:40,770
It's an opinion I share with some of the finest men I know.

517
00:37:40,960 --> 00:37:43,770
The secret history of the seven Kingdoms is written in these pages.

518
00:37:43,930 --> 00:37:46,250
Unless Lord Twatbeard made up a bunch of numbers

519
00:37:46,250 --> 00:37:47,730
to hide what he's really up to.

520
00:37:47,930 --> 00:37:49,640
Unless he made it all up, yes.

521
00:37:49,830 --> 00:37:51,730
- Podrick, do keep up. - Yes, my lord.

522
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:54,890
- Podrick. - Yes, my lord?

523
00:37:55,050 --> 00:37:57,660
After a long consultation with my colleague Ser Bronn,

524
00:37:58,150 --> 00:38:00,060
I finally found a suitable reward

525
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:02,200
for the services you've provided over and above

526
00:38:02,200 --> 00:38:03,910
what might be considered reasonable.

527
00:38:04,380 --> 00:38:05,390
Tell me, Pod,

528
00:38:07,730 --> 00:38:09,030
have you ever been with a woman?

529
00:38:09,550 --> 00:38:11,640
- No, my lord. - Wonderful.

530
00:38:13,110 --> 00:38:16,910
Genna specializes in first-timers.

531
00:38:17,860 --> 00:38:19,970
She's not bad with second-timers, either.

532
00:38:20,230 --> 00:38:21,870
Uh, my lord...

533
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:24,270
A fair enough repayment for putting your spear

534
00:38:24,270 --> 00:38:26,530
through my would-be killer's face, wouldn't you say?

535
00:38:28,630 --> 00:38:31,200
Now, as it happens,

536
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:33,340
Marei...

537
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:37,170
is quite the spear handler herself.

538
00:38:37,550 --> 00:38:40,720
She's here to thank you for staying by my side

539
00:38:40,970 --> 00:38:43,470
as the battle raged all around me.

540
00:38:44,150 --> 00:38:45,210
He's handsome.

541
00:38:46,850 --> 00:38:48,710
You didn't tell me he was handsome.

542
00:38:49,920 --> 00:38:53,770
Kayla is famous from here to Volantis

543
00:38:54,040 --> 00:38:56,300
in certain circles.

544
00:38:56,300 --> 00:38:58,670
One of four women in the world

545
00:38:58,900 --> 00:39:01,660
who can perform a proper Meereenese Knot.

546
00:39:02,640 --> 00:39:05,200
My lord, I don't even...

547
00:39:06,080 --> 00:39:07,350
She's here to thank you

548
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:09,740
for being a thoroughly respectful fellow

549
00:39:09,740 --> 00:39:12,840
who's never once failed to address me as "My lord."

550
00:39:15,950 --> 00:39:17,910
Be back in time for my supper.

551
00:39:22,630 --> 00:39:23,990
Pace yourself, lad.

552
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:28,890
For years I've heard that Littlefinger is a magician.

553
00:39:30,290 --> 00:39:33,260
Whenever the crown needs money, he rubs his hands together

554
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:36,030
and, poof, mountains of gold.

555
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:38,260
Let me guess. He's not a magician.

556
00:39:38,510 --> 00:39:39,190
No.

557
00:39:41,730 --> 00:39:44,650
- He's stealing it? - Worse, he's borrowing it.

558
00:39:45,970 --> 00:39:47,090
What's wrong with that?

559
00:39:47,620 --> 00:39:50,280
We can't afford to pay it back, that's what's wrong with it.

560
00:39:51,030 --> 00:39:53,220
The crown owes millions to my father.

561
00:39:53,390 --> 00:39:55,540
Seeing as it's his grandson's ass on the throne,

562
00:39:55,750 --> 00:39:57,430
I imagine he'll forgive that debt.

563
00:39:58,120 --> 00:40:00,330
Forgive a debt, my father?

564
00:40:00,510 --> 00:40:02,730
For a man of the world, you're strangely naive.

565
00:40:02,890 --> 00:40:04,540
I've never borrowed money before.

566
00:40:05,030 --> 00:40:06,500
I'm not clear on the rules.

567
00:40:06,500 --> 00:40:07,190
Well...

568
00:40:12,930 --> 00:40:14,870
The basic principle is

569
00:40:15,360 --> 00:40:16,620
I lend you money,

570
00:40:17,010 --> 00:40:19,080
and after an agreed upon period of time,

571
00:40:19,490 --> 00:40:22,050
you return it with interest.

572
00:40:22,540 --> 00:40:23,810
And what if I don't?

573
00:40:24,240 --> 00:40:25,450
Well, you have to.

574
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:27,190
But what if I don't?

575
00:40:27,460 --> 00:40:29,700
This is why I don't lend you money.

576
00:40:30,160 --> 00:40:32,890
Anyway, it's not my father I'm worried about.

577
00:40:32,890 --> 00:40:34,910
It's the Iron Bank of Braavos.

578
00:40:35,070 --> 00:40:37,040
We owe them tens of millions.

579
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:40,680
If we fail to repay these loans, the bank will fund our enemies.

580
00:40:41,310 --> 00:40:44,890
One way or another, they always get their gold back.

581
00:40:46,290 --> 00:40:48,920
Ah, the return of the conquering hero.

582
00:40:49,290 --> 00:40:51,200
Does he have a little jaunt in his step?

583
00:40:51,200 --> 00:40:52,920
The lad's practically skipping.

584
00:40:53,300 --> 00:40:54,670
You were gone a long time.

585
00:40:54,830 --> 00:40:56,370
I trust you got your money's worth.

586
00:40:56,370 --> 00:40:58,610
Or should I say my money's worth?

587
00:41:00,790 --> 00:41:03,070
Oh, it was a gift, Podrick.

588
00:41:03,560 --> 00:41:05,310
This is more than I give you in a year.

589
00:41:05,310 --> 00:41:07,380
He's a squire. You don't pay him.

590
00:41:07,560 --> 00:41:09,960
Oh, then it's much more than I give you in a year.

591
00:41:11,110 --> 00:41:12,600
They wouldn't take it, my lord.

592
00:41:13,980 --> 00:41:15,650
Maybe they're trying to curry some favor

593
00:41:15,650 --> 00:41:17,060
with the new Master of Coin.

594
00:41:17,060 --> 00:41:19,240
Have you ever known a whore to turn down gold?

595
00:41:19,630 --> 00:41:21,690
They were happy enough to take it when I gave it to them.

596
00:41:22,200 --> 00:41:23,120
What did you tell them?

597
00:41:24,190 --> 00:41:25,420
I didn't tell them anything.

598
00:41:25,420 --> 00:41:28,350
- What did you do to them? - Lots of things.

599
00:41:28,530 --> 00:41:30,830
And they seemed to like these things?

600
00:41:31,230 --> 00:41:32,030
Yes, my lord.

601
00:41:32,230 --> 00:41:33,470
Of course they seemed to like it.

602
00:41:33,470 --> 00:41:35,000
They're paid to seem to like it.

603
00:41:35,000 --> 00:41:36,710
Only they weren't paid.

604
00:41:37,470 --> 00:41:40,230
What are you saying? These ladies enjoyed him so much,

605
00:41:40,230 --> 00:41:41,950
they gave him the time for free?

606
00:41:43,070 --> 00:41:44,590
Is that what you're telling us?

607
00:41:49,150 --> 00:41:50,570
Sit down, Podrick.

608
00:41:56,370 --> 00:41:58,260
We're going to need details.

609
00:41:59,380 --> 00:42:01,580
Copious details.

610
00:42:42,020 --> 00:42:43,700
Come on! Come on!

611
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:46,790
Come on!

612
00:42:47,140 --> 00:42:48,000
Come on.

613
00:44:16,940 --> 00:44:18,650
You know how we deal with runaways here, do you?

614
00:44:26,630 --> 00:44:27,820
Take off his pants.

615
00:44:28,190 --> 00:44:29,930
No! No!

616
00:44:30,970 --> 00:44:32,060
No, no, no!

617
00:44:32,310 --> 00:44:34,370
I'm gonna fuck you into the dirt.

618
00:44:34,370 --> 00:44:36,840
No, please! No, no, no!

619
00:44:37,210 --> 00:44:38,630
No, don't!

620
00:44:40,620 --> 00:44:41,880
No, please!

621
00:44:43,160 --> 00:44:45,110
No! No!

622
00:45:14,510 --> 00:45:16,870
You little bastard.

623
00:45:26,930 --> 00:45:28,100
Come, my lord.

624
00:45:31,280 --> 00:45:34,440
You're a long way from home and winter is coming.

625
00:46:00,510 --> 00:46:02,270
I'll take the big bitch first.

626
00:46:02,760 --> 00:46:06,070
When she's good and wet, you lot can finish her off.

627
00:46:06,250 --> 00:46:08,790
My lord, I am Brienne of Tarth.

628
00:46:09,200 --> 00:46:11,070
Lady Catelyn Stark commanded me

629
00:46:11,070 --> 00:46:13,330
to deliver Ser Jaime to King's Landing.

630
00:46:13,530 --> 00:46:15,830
Catelyn Stark's a treasonous cunt.

631
00:46:16,200 --> 00:46:17,940
Orders were to take the Kingslayer alive.

632
00:46:17,940 --> 00:46:19,680
Nobody said shit about you.

633
00:46:25,210 --> 00:46:26,720
You're only making it worse.

634
00:46:31,300 --> 00:46:32,910
Take her over there where it's dark.

635
00:46:33,830 --> 00:46:35,220
I'd like a little privacy.

636
00:46:37,120 --> 00:46:37,970
Come on!

637
00:46:50,300 --> 00:46:52,040
You know who she is, don't you?

638
00:46:55,920 --> 00:46:58,190
A big dumb bitch from who cares where.

639
00:47:00,810 --> 00:47:02,330
Never been with a woman that big.

640
00:47:02,560 --> 00:47:04,030
She's Brienne of Tarth.

641
00:47:05,020 --> 00:47:07,390
Her father is Lord Selwyn Tarth.

642
00:47:08,540 --> 00:47:09,830
Heard of Tarth?

643
00:47:10,770 --> 00:47:12,060
I'm gonna cut your throat!

644
00:47:12,410 --> 00:47:14,930
They call it the Sapphire Isle. Do you know why?

645
00:47:16,350 --> 00:47:20,050
Every sapphire in Westeros was mined on Tarth.

646
00:47:21,270 --> 00:47:23,800
Sapphires are gemstones.

647
00:47:23,990 --> 00:47:26,140
- The blue ones. - I know what they are.

648
00:47:28,190 --> 00:47:30,680
Lord Selwyn would pay his daughter's weight

649
00:47:30,680 --> 00:47:32,990
in sapphires if she's returned to him.

650
00:47:34,180 --> 00:47:35,760
But only if she's alive,

651
00:47:36,910 --> 00:47:39,430
her honor unbesmirched.

652
00:47:50,130 --> 00:47:51,230
Bring her back here.

653
00:47:52,390 --> 00:47:53,500
Yes, my lord.

654
00:47:53,720 --> 00:47:55,150
- Get up. - Come on.

655
00:47:56,400 --> 00:47:58,370
Come on, on your feet.

656
00:47:59,800 --> 00:48:01,460
Unbesmirched.

657
00:48:03,730 --> 00:48:05,420
Not defiled.

658
00:48:05,980 --> 00:48:08,050
Fancy word for a fancy man.

659
00:48:09,900 --> 00:48:11,750
I hated to read as a child,

660
00:48:12,150 --> 00:48:14,940
but my father forced me to study the books every morning

661
00:48:14,940 --> 00:48:17,380
before I could practice with sword or horse.

662
00:48:18,220 --> 00:48:21,940
Two hours every day holed up in the maester's chambers.

663
00:48:22,240 --> 00:48:24,670
I learned a lot of fancy words.

664
00:48:25,470 --> 00:48:26,690
I bet you did.

665
00:48:35,720 --> 00:48:36,700
Your father,

666
00:48:37,950 --> 00:48:40,620
he'd pay your weight in gold to get you back?

667
00:48:42,180 --> 00:48:44,600
You'll be a rich man till the end of your days.

668
00:48:45,490 --> 00:48:46,780
And your sons will be rich men

669
00:48:46,780 --> 00:48:48,320
and their sons after them.

670
00:48:49,080 --> 00:48:51,160
Lands, titles--

671
00:48:51,950 --> 00:48:52,960
you'll have them all.

672
00:48:55,620 --> 00:48:58,030
The North can't win this war.

673
00:48:59,110 --> 00:49:00,970
You're a smart man. You understand that.

674
00:49:01,350 --> 00:49:03,050
We have the numbers. We have the gold.

675
00:49:03,050 --> 00:49:05,250
Aye, you have both.

676
00:49:06,560 --> 00:49:09,320
Fighting bravely for a losing cause

677
00:49:09,320 --> 00:49:10,860
is admirable.

678
00:49:11,770 --> 00:49:13,720
Fighting for a winning cause

679
00:49:14,410 --> 00:49:16,100
is far more rewarding.

680
00:49:17,410 --> 00:49:19,030
Hard to argue with that.

681
00:49:22,370 --> 00:49:24,990
Now that we're speaking together man to man,

682
00:49:25,440 --> 00:49:29,600
I wonder if you need to keep me chained to this tree.

683
00:49:30,030 --> 00:49:31,900
I'm not asking to be freed from my constraints,

684
00:49:31,900 --> 00:49:35,360
but if I could sleep lying down,

685
00:49:36,220 --> 00:49:38,080
my back would thank you for it.

686
00:49:38,630 --> 00:49:41,780
I'm not as young and resilient as I was once.

687
00:49:42,650 --> 00:49:43,950
None of us are.

688
00:49:47,350 --> 00:49:49,030
Unchain Ser Jaime from the tree.

689
00:49:49,030 --> 00:49:50,110
Come on, men.

690
00:49:57,160 --> 00:49:59,340
Suppose you'll be wanting something to eat.

691
00:50:00,700 --> 00:50:02,230
I'm famished, actually.

692
00:50:03,340 --> 00:50:05,300
I think we've got a spare partridge on the fire.

693
00:50:05,470 --> 00:50:07,210
Well, I do like partridge.

694
00:50:13,730 --> 00:50:17,360
Bring the bird over here and the carving knife.

695
00:50:21,110 --> 00:50:23,660
Will this work as a table, my lord?

696
00:50:24,120 --> 00:50:25,680
Oh, yes. Yes, this will do nic--

697
00:50:27,450 --> 00:50:28,520
On the stump.

698
00:50:35,360 --> 00:50:38,720
You think you're the smartest man there is.

699
00:50:41,100 --> 00:50:44,200
That everyone alive has to bow and scrape

700
00:50:44,200 --> 00:50:45,920
and lick your boots.

701
00:50:46,540 --> 00:50:48,570
- My father-- - And if you get in any trouble,

702
00:50:48,570 --> 00:50:51,500
all you've got to do is say "My father"

703
00:50:51,890 --> 00:50:54,590
and that's it, all your troubles are gone.

704
00:50:56,670 --> 00:50:59,120
- Don't. - Have you got something to say?

705
00:51:03,650 --> 00:51:06,780
Careful. You don't want to say the wrong thing.

706
00:51:09,830 --> 00:51:12,290
You're nothing without your daddy,

707
00:51:12,790 --> 00:51:14,820
and your daddy ain't here.

708
00:51:16,290 --> 00:51:17,930
Never forget that.

709
00:51:20,340 --> 00:51:22,430
Here, this should help you remember.

710
00:51:30,410 --> 00:51:32,710
*A bear there was, a bear, a bear!*

711
00:51:33,780 --> 00:51:36,410
*All black and brown and covered with hair!*

712
00:51:37,450 --> 00:51:40,020
*Three boys, a goat, and a dancing bear!*

713
00:51:40,910 --> 00:51:43,440
*They danced and spun all the way to the Fair!*

714
00:51:44,560 --> 00:51:46,890
*How sweet she was, and pure, and fair!*

715
00:51:48,210 --> 00:51:50,400
*The maid with honey in her hair!*

716
00:51:51,840 --> 00:51:54,150
*He smelled the scent on the summer air.*

717
00:51:55,440 --> 00:51:57,890
*The maid with honey in her hair!*

718
00:51:58,660 --> 00:52:02,190
*From there to here. From here to there!*

719
00:52:02,380 --> 00:52:05,760
*All black and brown, and covered with hair!*

720
00:52:06,220 --> 00:52:09,530
*He smelled the scent on the summer air.*

721
00:52:09,690 --> 00:52:13,480
- *The bear, the bear.* - *The maiden fair.*

722
00:52:13,680 --> 00:52:16,370
*Oh, I'm a maid, and I'm pure and fair!*

723
00:52:17,340 --> 00:52:19,850
*I'll never dance with a hairy bear!*

724
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:23,300
*I called for a knight, but you're a bear!*

725
00:52:24,800 --> 00:52:27,220
*All black and brown and covered with hair!*

726
00:52:28,000 --> 00:52:31,540
*From there to here. From here to there!*

727
00:52:31,730 --> 00:52:35,270
*All black and brown and covered with hair!*

728
00:52:35,610 --> 00:52:38,920
*He smelled the scent on the summer air.*

729
00:52:39,070 --> 00:52:42,680
- *The bear, the bear.* - *The maiden fair.*

